 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A pyramid is a solid with one base (a polygon) and triangular lateral faces that meet at a point called the apex.\nIn a regular pyramid, the base is a regular polygon and the apex is directly above the centre of the base.\nVocabulary: base, lateral faces (triangles), apex, edges, vertices; the altitude (height) is the perpendicular distance from the apex to the base; the slant height is the height of a lateral triangle, from the apex to the middle of a base edge.\nA pyramid is named after its base: triangular, square, pentagonal, hexagonal pyramid.\nFor an n-sided base: n + 1 faces, 2n edges, n + 1 vertices.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": 4
  },
  "problem": "How many faces, edges and vertices does a square pyramid have?",
  "steps": [
   "The base has {n} sides.",
   "Faces = n + {1} = {pyramidFaces(n)}; edges = {2}n = {pyramidEdges(n)}; vertices = n + {1} = {pyramidVertices(n)}."
  ],
  "answer": "{pyramidFaces(n)} faces, {pyramidEdges(n)} edges, {pyramidVertices(n)} vertices",
  "checks": [],
  "figure": "pyramidFig(n)"
 },
